                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5ac7188e-72cb-3483-8597-9dc057d8b11c",
                        "entry_id": "a7aa5e28-9320-3a8f-97a2-6565a24e8dc4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Nom que donnent les adeptes de l'ufologie aux personnes qui doutent de l'existence des OVNI."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "pejorative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "eeb53efe-9306-43af-909d-3d845dd8934e",
                                "sense_id": "5ac7188e-72cb-3483-8597-9dc057d8b11c",
                                "text": "L’hypothèse qui a motivé la rédaction du présent texte, et qui sera développée plus bas, a germé à l’occasion de la relecture du cas Arnold vu par « le pélicaniste » James Easton. Ainsi nomme-t-on, souvent ironiquement du côté « tenant », ceux qui ont proposé comme hypothèse (théorie ?) explicative de l’observation de K.A un vol de neuf pélicans."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f310da82-b4c3-4d50-9d31-69291e4b3787",
                                "sense_id": "5ac7188e-72cb-3483-8597-9dc057d8b11c",
                                "text": "Une autre “ explication ” a encore surgi en 1999 sur internet, celle d’une confusion avec un vol de ...pélicans ! L’auteur en est encore un Anglais, James Easton, qui s’est taillé une solide réputation internationale de sceptique. […]. Un ufologue américain réputé, Jerome Clark, a proposé, en guise de conclusion à cette comédie, de parler désormais de « pélicanistes » et de “ pélicanisation ” pour toute explication farfelue de cet acabit."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b12e9802-ab75-43c9-bf55-34073b81d982",
                        "source_entry_id": "a7aa5e28-9320-3a8f-97a2-6565a24e8dc4",
                        "source_sense_id": "5ac7188e-72cb-3483-8597-9dc057d8b11c",
                        "target_lang_word": "pelicanist",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "pelicanist",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "35f8a798-9a9d-3869-9fd6-e2102866d1b8",
                                        "entry_id": "4fc2c40a-2fc6-381e-a97a-b4b459a32e3f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Someone who attempts to explain away UFO reports by means of any explanation, however illogical."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c190bb43-cae8-4ec9-ba81-f4ea65bdbc3f",
                                                "sense_id": "35f8a798-9a9d-3869-9fd6-e2102866d1b8",
                                                "text": "Kottmeyer and others who use the psychosocial hypothesis constantly produce evidence to support their ideas, whereas the ETH proponents can produce only cranky ideas about magnetic, anti-gravity or interdimensional saucers, accompanied by a constant whining about \"sceptics\" and \"debunkers\" (not to mention \"armchair ufologists\" and \"pelicanists\")."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0333833c-8d59-4306-a99d-4383dc964f35",
                                                "sense_id": "35f8a798-9a9d-3869-9fd6-e2102866d1b8",
                                                "text": "Meanwhile, those of us who’ve experienced UFOs in various ways are called mentally ill, intellectually challenged, spiritually needy, liars, drunks, drug users, attention getters, drama queens, and more. Those all are worse than being called a thug, skeptibunkie, or Pelicanist. (Pelicanist is a term coined by UFO researcher Jerome Clark.)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d57ff0b5-4d02-4bdf-93bc-2562b2eeaa8b",
                                                "sense_id": "35f8a798-9a9d-3869-9fd6-e2102866d1b8",
                                                "text": "As opposed to... Skepticism of the pelicanist flavor perhaps ?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
